        {
          "ruleId": "ignored-gdn-tests-move-to-nightly-only",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "The six kernels::gdn device tests lose PR-path coverage (cuda-unit runs plain cargo test, which skips #[ignore]d tests) and run only in the cuda-nightly ada-yoga job. The names in the new step were checked against the sources: all six exist, each is a #[test] with the new #[ignore], they sit in #[cfg(feature = cuda)] modules under kernels::gdn::{decode_attention,decode_attention_split,delta_rule_scan} (mods declared in gdn/mod.rs), and the two timing probes in decode_attention_split.rs already carry their own #[ignore], which --exact keeps out. No other workflow or ci/sections.yml step runs these with --include-ignored (grep), and the Sigma lists in sections.yml run without the cuda feature, so no count gate sees the six as unexecuted. The new step is the only executor; the commit message discloses this and #4956 owns it."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": ".github/workflows/cuda-nightly.yml"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 855
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "measured",
            "failure_scenario": "If the ada-yoga job is skipped or red earlier in the night, the six GDN bit-identity tests run nowhere; a real GDN regression merged meanwhile is caught a night late.",
            "precision_class": "advisory",
            "command": [
              "git",
              "grep",
              "-n",
              "-E",
              "include-ignored|--ignored",
              "--",
              ".github/workflows",
              "ci/sections.yml"
            ],
            "exit_code": 0
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "new-step-shell-semantics",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Read, not executed (cargo test is forbidden on this box). Under set -euo pipefail, cmd > log 2>&1 || rc=$? captures the status and does not trip -e; tail runs on every path; RUNNER_TEMP is defined by the runner and ci/sections.yml uses it the same way; $g::decode_attention::x expands $g then the literal text (a colon is not a name character); the unquoted words split into six test filters. libtest prints a line starting test result: ok. 6 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; N filtered out with --ignored --exact, so the anchored grep -q holds, and 16 passed would not match. if: always() makes the step run after an earlier step failed or the job was cancelled, which suits an independent nightly signal and can only add a red, never hide one. Residual: the tests print SKIPPED and return when CudaContext::new(0) fails, which libtest counts as passed, so six passes do not prove the device was used; the preflight step in the job (sm_89, GPU lock) is what covers that. timeout-minutes 30 to 45 is not measured here (no run). No defect found."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": ".github/workflows/cuda-nightly.yml"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 855
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "asserted",
            "failure_scenario": "On a runner whose device vanished mid-job the step would print SKIPPED six times, pass, and report 6 passed; a driver fault that makes CudaContext::new fail turns the nightly green without running a kernel.",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "cuda-docs/claim-not-grounded",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Claim 1: concurrent multi-thread context or stream use can crash libcuda. The CUDA documentation returned (driver API contexts, current context per thread) is silent on crashes under concurrent use; no excerpt supports it. The cause stays an empirical hypothesis."
